<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=pendulum-clock">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pendulum clock</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A pendulum clock is a clock that uses a swinging weight, the pendulum, as its timekeeping element; it was invented on 25 December 1656 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a>, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>'s investigations of pendulums, and patented the following year. The pendulum improved the accuracy of clocks from about 15 minutes to 15 seconds a day, and many existing verge-and-foliot clocks were fitted with one. Pendulum clocks remained the world standard for accurate timekeeping until the invention of the quartz clock in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pendulum-clock.jpg" alt="Pendulum clock" width="320" height="913" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christiaan Huygens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huygens_first_pendulum_clock.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
